append content
  section#introduction
    +h(2, "Introduction")
    p.
      This project is dedicated to my grandfather, #[+link("https://voormeij.ca/") Peter John Voormeij], who passed away
      in early 2024. He was a Dutch painter and printmaker, and I wanted to explore and honour his abstract artwork in
      my own way. Thanks to this
      #[+link("https://www.sfu.ca/outlines.html?2025/spring/iat/460/d100") Generative AI and Computational Creativity]
      course with #[+link("https://www.metacreation.net/philippepasquier/home") Dr. Philippe Pasquier] at Simon Fraser
      University (#[abbr SFU]), I found the perfect opportunity! With the blessing of my family, I constructed a dataset
      of ~360 works and began brainstorming. I wanted to explore the shapes, patterns, and colours found in his work and
      create an interactive experience that enables users to do the same.
    p.
      Having recently completed a Unity-based virtual reality (#[abbr VR]) course,
      #[+link("https://www.sfu.ca/outlines.html?2024/fall/iat/445/d100") Immersive Environments], I found myself wanting
      to dive deeper and further develop my skills in that area. With an improved understanding of what makes for an
      engaging experience, I ended up settling on #[abbr VR] for the user interaction half of the project.
    p.
      As the course progressed, it became clear that #[dfn generative adversarial networks] (#[abbr GANs]) would be a
      powerful option, as they run faster than
      #[+link("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diffusion_model") diffusion models] (e.g., Midjourney) and don't require
      nearly as much data. Essentially, #[abbr GANs] work by pitting two separate neural networks against each
      other in an arms race: a #[dfn generator] network and a #[dfn discriminator] network. The #[em generator's] job is
      to create new images from random noise, and the #[em discriminator] tries to tell apart the generator's fake
      output from the real training data. Both networks learn from their mistakes and try to improve at their assigned
      tasks over time. If everything goes perfectly, the discriminator ends up with a 50% chance of making the correct
      prediction, meaning the generator's images are impossible for it to tell apart from the real images.

  section#literature-review
    +h(2, "Literature Review")
    p.
      Enter #[+link("https://www.metacreation.net/autolume") Autolume-Live], a tool for training, tweaking, and running
      #[abbr GAN] models locally in real-time. Initially created for
      #[+link("https://summit.sfu.ca/item/36414") Jonas Kraasch's Master's thesis], it has continued to see development,
      improvements, and usage thanks to the #[+link("https://www.metacreation.net/") Metacreation Lab].
    p.
      An in-class training workshop from PhD student
      #[+link("https://www.metacreation.net/team/arshia-sobhan") Arshia Sobhan] truly revealed the capabilities of the
      software, highlighting the real-time image generation with a wide array of options and vectors to explore, all
      configurable through #[dfn #[+link("https://opensoundcontrol.stanford.edu/") Open Sound Control]] (#[abbr OSC]),
      a data communication protocol between computers and multimedia devices. Given the abstract nature of my dataset,
      working with Autolume's unique visual style felt like the right approach for the project.
    p.
      Autolume also transmits its generated output to a #[dfn #[+link("https://ndi.video/") Network Device Interface]]
      (#[abbr NDI]), another communication standard that streams video and audio to connected multimedia devices.
    p.
      During the proposal peer review process, I was introduced to a
      #[+link("https://youtu.be/zyFAGmBXX90") demo video by Hobby Technology] of a project with a similar concept, also
      using a #[abbr VR] headset to manipulate the vectors of a latent space. While it featured a different type of
      model, the user interface served as an inspiration and a confirmation that my initial idea was feasible.

  section#system-design
    +h(2, "System Design")
    p.
      I borrowed a Meta Quest headset through #[abbr SFU]'s library and designed the system around it. Fortunately,
      thanks to the
      #[+link("https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.xr.interaction.toolkit@3.1/manual/index.html") XR Interaction Toolkit],
      other headsets are easily supportable with little extra effort!
    p.
      On the surface, AutolumeGallery is fairly simple and, apart from the
      #[a(href="#vr-hardware") #[strong #[abbr VR] Hardware]], is divided into two components:
      #[a(href="#unity") #[strong Unity]] and #[a(href="#autolume-live") #[strong Autolume-Live]]. Between them, the
      information flows and repeats as follows:

    ul
      li #[strong Unity] receives user input from the #[strong #[abbr VR] Hardware] and in-world interactables.
      li #[strong Unity] uses #[abbr OSC] to send decimals to #[strong Autolume-Live].
        ul
          li #[strong Unity] receives user input from the #[strong #[abbr VR] Hardware] and in-world interactables.
          li #[strong Unity] uses #[abbr OSC] to send decimals to #[strong Autolume-Live].
          li #[strong Autolume-Live] receives #[abbr OSC] output and adjusts the corresponding #[abbr GAN] settings.
          li #[strong Autolume-Live] uses #[abbr NDI] to broadcast generated images back to #[strong Unity].
          li #[strong Unity] receives #[abbr NDI] output and updates the virtual canvas.
      li #[strong Autolume-Live] receives #[abbr OSC] output and adjusts the corresponding #[abbr GAN] settings.
      li #[strong Autolume-Live] uses #[abbr NDI] to broadcast generated images back to #[strong Unity].
      li #[strong Unity] receives #[abbr NDI] output and updates the virtual canvas.

  section#implementation-details
    +h(2, "Implementation Details")
    p.
      The current implementation requires #[strong Unity] and #[strong Autolume-Live] to be running on the same machine,
      as they both look to different ports on the local IP address. Detailed installation instructions can be found on
      the #[+link("https://github.com/rdezwart/AutolumeGallery") GitHub repository] for this project.

    section#vr-hardware
      +h(3, "VR Hardware")
      p.
        The Meta Quest is connected to a computer using the
        #[+link("https://www.meta.com/help/quest/1517439565442928/") Meta Quest Link] application, either over Wi-Fi or
        #[abbr USB] cable. From there, the desktop link feature is used to mirror the computer's display to the headset.

    section#unity
      +h(3, "Unity")
      p.
        The heart of the system. Relies on #[+link("https://www.khronos.org/openxr/") OpenXR] and the
        #[+link("https://docs.unity3d.com/Packages/com.unity.xr.interaction.toolkit@3.1/manual/index.html") XR Interaction Toolkit]
        for the baseline virtual reality functionality. Other notable external libraries included
        #[+link("https://github.com/Iam1337/extOSC") extOSC] and #[+link("https://github.com/keijiro/KlakNDI") KlakNDI],
        both of which greatly aided the process of connecting to and from Autolume.
      p.
        There are comments in every script I wrote linking to any external resources I drew from.
      p.
        The hierarchy in #[+figure-link(counters["Figure"] + 1)] shows the game objects in a collapsed state, as they
        have too many children to show. I will run through each object and any relevant children.

      +figure()
        +picture(new ProjectImage({
          name: "hierarchy",
          width: 286,
          height: 239,
          alt: "Unity hierarchy for this project.",
          fallbackFormat: "png",
          small: false
        }))
        +figcaption() Root hierarchy of the Gallery scene.

      section#directional-light
        +h(4, "Directional Light")
        p.
          Standard Unity asset providing sunlight to the scene.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p #[strong Libraries:] (none)

      section#xr-interaction-manager
        +h(4, "XR Interaction Manager")
        p.
          Responsible for handling and delegating interaction triggers and events around the scene. Collaborates with
          the #[a(href="#xr-origin") #[code XR Origin]].
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#osc-manager
        +h(4, "OSC Manager")
        p.
          Responsible for transmitting and receiving information over OSC. The transmitter connects to
          #[code 127.0.0.1:1338] and is configured to send its packets in bundles instead of individually. It does
          not trigger any of the transmissions itself, but will diligently send off any messages it's given.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li extOSC

      section#xr-origin
        +h(4, "XR Origin (XR Rig) Affordance Variant")
        p.
          Contains the VR controller models, the player camera, locomotion controllers, and interactors of various
          types. This object is the user's gateway into the scene, being the digital equivalent of the user's physical
          hardware.
        p.
          The locomotion controllers are how the player can move around the scene. Types include #[dfn Turn] (rotating
          the camera a fixed amount with the joystick), #[dfn Move] (walking around with the joystick), and
          #[dfn Teleportation] (blinking the user to the target location).
        p.
          The interactor children also have various specifications, such as #[dfn Gaze] (where the user is looking),
          #[dfn Poke] (what the user is touching), #[dfn Near-Far] (what the user is pointing at), and #[dfn Teleport]
          (where the user wants to go).
        p.
          There are also #[dfn Affordance Callouts] attached to the controller, which are in charge of displaying the
          button mappings when the user looks at their controller (we can thank the #[dfn Gaze] interactor for this).
          These affordances are lifted from one of Unity's template VR starter projects.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#painting
        +h(4, "Painting")
        p.
          The digital canvas Autolume is sending artwork to. There are four #[dfn Painting Panel] manipulator children,
          each has an #[abbr NDI] receiver updating their texture, and a socket interactor that will grab and hold onto
          Panels. The user can grab, move, rotate, and flip each panel. The #[abbr NDI] receivers are listening for
          "Autolume Live" on the local machine.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom,
            li KlakNDI,
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#vector-controllers
        +h(4, "Vector Controller Left/Right")
        p.
          Manipulator spheres floating above reset podiums. They can be grabbed by the user and can move in all axes,
          but cannot rotate. They send their #[var x], #[var y], and #[var z] positions to Autolume via the OSC Manager.
          There are affordances labelling each podium.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom,
            li extOSC,
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#layer-controller
        +h(4, "Layer Controller")
        p.
          A directional manipulator wheel floating above a reset podium. It can be grabbed by the user, can move along
          the #[var x],#[var y] axes, and can rotate around the #[var z] axis. It sends its #[var x], #[var y] position
          and #[var z] rotation to Autolume via the OSC Manager. There is an affordance labelling this podium.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom,
            li extOSC,
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#seed-controller
        +h(4, "Seed Controller")
        p.
          A directional manipulator wheel floating above a reset podium. It can be grabbed by the user, can move along
          the #[var x] axis, and can rotate around the #[var z] axis. It sends its #[var x] position and #[var z]
          rotation to Autolume via the OSC Manager. There is an affordance labelling this podium.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom,
            li extOSC,
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#panel-resetter
        +h(4, "Panel Resetter")
        p.
          A reset podium without a manipulator. When pressed, it resets the Painting Panels to their original
          positions and orientations. There is an affordance labelling this podium.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom,
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#environment
        +h(4, "Environment")
        p.
          Contains objects such as the floor and their #[dfn Teleport Areas], as well as the introductory text callout
          when the experience launches.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li XR Interaction Toolkit

      section#camera
        +h(4, "Camera")
        p.
          A second, disabled camera responsible for taking photos of the canvas. When triggered, it renders what it
          can see to a #[code RenderTexture] and converts it to a #[code Texture2D], which is then saved as a
          #[code .png] in the application's data folder.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom

      section#quit-listener
        +h(4, "QuitListener")
        p.
          Empty game object with a simple script to quit the application when the #[kbd ESC] key is pressed.
        div.libraries.secondary
          p: strong Libraries:
          ul.comma-list(role="list")
            li Custom

    section#autolume-live
      +h(3, "Autolume-Live")
      p.
        With the guidance of Arshia Sobhan, he and I used one of the Metacreation Lab's computers to train a model on
        my grandfather's artwork over the course of several days. He kept an eye on the training process while he was
        on campus, and eventually a suitable model emerged.
      p.
        Once transferred to my home computer, I used Autolume's #[dfn Feature Extractor] to find six meaningful latent
        space vectors. I chose vectors that created interesting changes in the artwork and was excited for users to
        explore.
      p.
        I created a preset containing the trained model, my chosen vectors, and several parameters listening to
        different OSC channels:

      //- TODO: RESUME EDITING FROM AROUND HERE, AND MAYBE MAKE `VAR` BOLD

      ul
        li Latent Seed (#[code seed/latent]): Seed Controller's #[var x] position
        li Noise Seed (#[code seed/noise]): Seed Controller's #[var z] rotation
        li Vector 1 (#[code left/x]): Vector Controller Left's #[var x] position
        li Vector 2 (#[code left/y]): Vector Controller Left's #[var y] position
        li Vector 3 (#[code left/z]): Vector Controller Left's #[var z] position
        li Vector 4 (#[code right/x]): Vector Controller Right's #[var x] position
        li Vector 5 (#[code right/y]): Vector Controller Right's #[var y] position
        li Vector 6 (#[code right/z]): Vector Controller Right's #[var z] position
        li Layer 3, Translation (#[code layer/x]): Layer Controller's #[var x] position
        li Layer 3, Translation (#[code layer/y]): Layer Controller's #[var y] position
        li Layer 3, Rotation (#[code layer/r]): Layer Controller's #[var z] rotation
        li Layer 3, Scale (#[code layer/s]): Layer Controller's #[var x] scale

  section#results
    +h(2, "Results")
    +figure()
      +picture(new ProjectImage({
        name: "canvas-3",
        width: 512,
        height: 512,
        alt: "A composition saved from AutolumeGallery, primarily blues and greens."
      }))
      +figcaption("canvas-3").
        The output of all four canvas panels tiled next to each other. The bottom left panel is unmodified, showing
        Autolume's output in the original orientation. By default, the rest of the tiles are always flipped in this
        combination.

    p.
      During the early stages of development, I only had a single "Painting Panel". It directly matched the output
      from Autolume-Live and was great for prototyping and developing the code base! However, it didn't quite have the
      visual interest or interactivity I was looking for.
    p.
      See #[+figure-link(counters["Figure"])]. If you like, use your hands to cover three-quarters of the image, leaving
      just the bottom left corner clear. You're now seeing what essentially was the original single-panel setup. I found
      it still interesting, but felt it was missing\ldots something. Now, uncover the rest of the image and notice how
      different the piece feels. To me, there's more complexity, feels unified, and is overall more aesthetically
      pleasing.
    p.
      Increasing the panel count and allowing users to change the structure of the piece itself was a huge realization
      and breakthrough for the project. It significantly increased the creative possibilities and opened many new
      doors for interactions and development directions. I love how the user is able to take a much more active role
      in the creation of the piece, and how their decisions become a part of the artwork, if only for a little while.
    p.
      The Autolume model we trained is limited to a resolution of 512x512 pixels, which didn't turn out to be as much
      of an issue as I initially thought. It's significantly more noticeable the closer you are to a panel, but from a
      distance it's negligible.

  section#conclusion-future-work
    +h(2, "Conclusion &amp; Future Work")
    p.
      I am very happy and proud of the current state of the project, and I look forward to being able to share the
      actual experience, not just a demo video. I plan to continue training the model and looking at other directions
      to take the dataset once the semester is over.
    p.
      There were some additional approaches to visualizing latent spaces that I found during my preliminary research
      phase, but unfortunately did not get to implement due to scope creep and a lack of time
      (#[+citation("Despois", "2017")]; #[+citation("van der Maaten &amp; Hinton", "2008")]). I'd love to explore the
      visualization possibilities of latent spaces more in the future, especially under the guidance of the Metacreation
      Lab.
    p.
      Brought up as feedback during the class presentation, I struggled to find clear information on the concept of
      "intermediate latent fields", which was a potential way of smoothing the harsh panel boundaries when textures
      no longer line up. I want to give this a shot, but need some help.

  section#ethical-statement
    +h(2, "Ethical Statement")
    p.
      At the end of the day, I am not a fan of models trained on stolen data, or when people try to pass off AI art
      as their own human art; the original authors/artists deserve credit and compensation.
    p.
      That's why it's so important to me that I can train models locally with Autolume; I know with absolute certainty
      where the data is coming from and how the model has been created. The dataset of my grandfather’s art belongs to
      my family and always will. I am infinitely more comfortable generating from it knowing that I am in full control
      and can give it the proper credit and respect it deserves.

  section#acknowledgements
    +h(2, "Acknowledgements")
    p.
      With this course nearing its end, I am extremely grateful for my professor Philippe Pasquier and teaching
      assistant Griffin Page, I have had a wonderful time and learned so much this semester. I am very excited to see
      what the future holds for the work being done at the Metacreation Lab, and hope to contribute some myself!
    p.
      I also want to give my endless thanks to Arshia Sobhan for guiding me through the Autolume model training
      process and lending me time on his lab machine to get the training done. My computer at home is nowhere near
      strong enough, and he saved the day.
    p.
      Lastly, I want to acknowledge my grandfather. I've had so much fun working on this project and hope you're proud
      of what I've got so far. Love and miss you, Opa.

